# AIL 0.2 Surface Grammar

```ebnf
program     = { statement } ;
statement   = observe | unknown | derive | relate | decide | authorize | execute ;
observe     = "OBSERVE" , subject , "=" , value , "@" , evidence-id ;
unknown     = "UNKNOWN" , subject ;
derive      = "DERIVE" , claim-id , subject , "=" , value , "FROM" , claim-list ;
relate      = "RELATE" , edge-id , subject , "-[" , predicate , "]->" , subject , "@" , evidence-id ;
decide      = "DECIDE" , action-id , text ;
authorize   = "AUTHORIZE" , action-id , "BY" , principal ;
execute     = "EXECUTE" , action-id , "RESULT" , text ;

subject     = token ;
predicate   = token ;
principal   = token ;
claim-id    = "CL-" , digit , digit , digit ;
evidence-id = "EV-" , digit , digit , digit ;
edge-id     = "ED-" , digit , digit , digit ;
action-id   = "ACT-" , digit , digit , digit ;
value       = json-value | text ;
claim-list  = claim-id , { "," , claim-id } ;
text        = { character } ;
token       = { letter | digit | ":" | "_" | "." | "-" } ;
```

Semantics:
- OBSERVE creates provenance-bearing observed evidence.
- UNKNOWN creates an explicit knowledge gap.
- DERIVE creates a derived claim from prior claims.
- RELATE creates a graph edge and requires evidence provenance.
- DECIDE creates intent only; it does not authorize execution.
- AUTHORIZE is the explicit permission boundary.
- EXECUTE is valid only after authorization.

The surface language is deliberately small. Rich JSON remains the canonical transport representation.